Stop a dialog being snapped wider than the screen it is shown on.

BubbleFrameView snaps any bubble that has buttons to one of Chrome's dialog
widths. The smallest of those is wider than this port's phone screen, so the
Bluetooth device chooser was laid out 462dp wide on a 376dp work area, its
right edge past the display and its Connect button somewhere no touch could
reach. Measured on device, with the chooser's own contents already clamped to
312dp: contents_preferred=312x320, work_area=0,0 376x809, bubble bounds
34,-4 462x483. The width was not coming from the contents.

Snapping is a nicety. Fitting on the screen is not, on a device where there is
nowhere else for the dialog to go. The snapped width is now capped at the work
area less a small margin, and min_width still wins over the cap -- a frame
narrower than its own contents is not a size, it is a broken layout, and the
contents-side clamp in ohos-device-chooser-width.patch is what keeps the two
from meeting there.

This is deliberately not limited to the chooser. Every dialog with buttons is
snapped by this function, and none of them fit either.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-dialog-width-fits-screen.patch

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/views/chrome_layout_provider.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/views/chrome_layout_provider.cc
index 7814bc0..3dcdbc5 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/chrome_layout_provider.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/chrome_layout_provider.cc
@@ -10,7 +10,10 @@
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/ui_features.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/views/chrome_typography.h"
 #include "components/omnibox/common/omnibox_features.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "ui/base/pointer/touch_ui_controller.h"
+#include "ui/display/display.h"
+#include "ui/display/screen.h"
 #include "ui/gfx/shadow_value.h"
 
 namespace {
@@ -298,14 +301,32 @@ int ChromeLayoutProvider::GetDistanceMetric(int metric) const {
 }
 
 int ChromeLayoutProvider::GetSnappedDialogWidth(int min_width) const {
+  int width = ((min_width + 15) / 16) * 16;
   for (int snap_point :
        {kSmallDialogWidth, kMediumDialogWidth, kLargeDialogWidth}) {
     if (min_width <= snap_point) {
-      return snap_point;
+      width = snap_point;
+      break;
     }
   }
 
-  return ((min_width + 15) / 16) * 16;
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // The snap points are desktop widths and the smallest of them is wider than
+  // this port's phone screen, so a dialog with buttons was laid out past the
+  // right edge of the display -- far enough that the Bluetooth chooser's
+  // Connect button could not be touched at all. Snapping is a nicety; fitting
+  // on the screen is not. min_width still wins, because a frame narrower than
+  // its own contents is not a size, it is a broken layout.
+  if (const display::Screen* screen = display::Screen::Get()) {
+    constexpr int kScreenEdgeMargin = 16;
+    width = std::max(
+        min_width,
+        std::min(width, screen->GetPrimaryDisplay().work_area().width() -
+                            kScreenEdgeMargin));
+  }
+#endif
+
+  return width;
 }
 
